    {
      "id": "d7dd553643bd9a9c0b2ffc0a239ac7a58e891290",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2132236315,
          "type": null,
          "body": "This one's a bit weird.\n\nThe `ws_return_val_if()` test expands to something similar to (with an extra call in the `if` statement)\n\n```\n    do {\n        if (WS_ASSERT_ENABLED && (size < 3))\n            return NULL;\n    } while (0);\n```\n\nIf I replace the call with that code, change `WS_ASSERT_ENABLED` to `WS_HELLO_ENABLED`, #define `WS_HELLO_ENABLED` to 0, remove the `_U_` for the `size` argument, and compile it with clang on my Mac, I get no unused argument warning.\n\nIf, however, I #define `WS_HELLO_ENABLED` to (0), and compile, I get errors complaining that the `(size < 3)` test isn't being executed.\n\nGiven that *wrapping the 0 in parentheses* provokes the errors, this looks like a *very* specialized hack in clang - and, I suspect, GCC, given that we didn't get warnings from this until we cranked up the warning level in MSVC, which presumably ''doesn't'' have the hack.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-09-26T22:01:27.705Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-09-26T22:01:27.705Z",
